tunity to make an alibi at 9 pm. Things are not as simple as Wakatsuki-san said. [4096] \"I think your idea is good, but this conclusion still does not seem to point to who the killer is!\" Miyuki's statement was very mild, giving Wang Yuezhou a pat on the back for his good performance. [4097] \"Although it's a pity, it seems to be the case.\" It seems that Wang Yuezhou also admitted it, \"However, I think the suspect range should be narrowed down.\" [4098] \"So, I and Wang Yuezhou and others are also in the range of suspicion.\" Yoshiro thought of this, \"And those who have no alibis at 7 o'clock and 9 o'clock, Mr. Nishii and Miss Bao, are outside the range. This is strange, isn't it? Er, I'm not trying to drag people who have been cleared of suspicion back into the circle of suspicion... but... it seems that being alone and having no half-baked alibis is still better!\" [4099] \"My head is spinning!\" Miyuki put her hand on her forehead as if to measure the temperature. [4100] 2\n[4101] \"It would be better to tell the police about the content of our conversation so far. I think Mr. Wangyue's pointing out that the note was put in the right back pocket by the one with a sore right shoulder has great value!\" [4102] After Yushi said this, Wangyue modestly said, \"The police will notice this point soon. They may not remember how sore the right shoulder of the one who put the note in the right back pocket was - it's hard to remember it all at once if you haven't seen it yourself.\" [4103] \"But, we've already discovered that point! I want to go all out to identify the real culprit!\" Yutaka regretted. [4104] To let the brain rest, a series of random chit-chat began. [4105] Yushi showed off his loquaciousness, \"If you think about it carefully, you can't underestimate the children. Their ability to read between the lines is like adults exchanging glances, and if you underestimate them...\n[4106] He seemed very enthusiastic and went on and on about his experiences of children catching him in his lies. I was tired of listening to others talk and half-heartedly listened. [4107] \"There are also opposite examples, overestimating children is not right. Although there is a saying that 'things that can be seen in childhood cannot be seen when one becomes an adult', I don't agree. Childhood dreams are naive things because children don't have the ability to achieve their dreams. It is adults who truly become deeply immersed in dreams, and they often cannot forget their vague childhood dreams for life! Dreams\u2026\"\n[4108] Yashima's voice gradually became distant. I returned to thinking about the truth of Sugawara's murder. [4109] \"The assumption that the killer had no alibi at 7 PM and had an alibi at 9 PM can be established. However, is this reasoning really accurate? Even if the killer put the note in the corpse's pocket, the argument that '7' was changed to '9' is rather weak. It doesn't seem natural that Sugawara had a two and a half hour blank period if the meeting time was 9 PM. On the surface, it seems reasonable, but I lack decisive evidence to explain the unnatural feeling. Perhaps Sugawara really spent those two and a half hours looking for a shooting target and wandering around. Shouldn't we focus more on whether the alibi at 7 PM or 9 PM is better? Yashima's voice seemed to whisper in my ear, and I could barely hear it. [4110] \"Hearing Mr. Nishimura's dream of visiting Enoshima is also like this feeling. It's a dream of adults who are lonely and bored, a dream they had to imagine out of necessity.\" [4111] Whether it's the alibi at 7 pm or 9 pm, do we have to go back to a blank state? No\u2026 From just now, a certain thought has been tugging at my thoughts. It seems that a question seeking an answer has been calmed down by someone's words - although I'm not sure if it was that person who said it. I remember what it was, but I have a feeling of a major hint and even seem to feel the presence of a residual image. [4112] \"So, does that mean that the power of dreams becomes art when accompanied by technology?\" [4113] \"Yes, that's right. It's better to be a quiet wise man than a talkative fool. If you think about it that way, you can say that artists are nothing more than orators!\" [4114] \"Art and language should not be of equal value, right?\" [4115] \"Yes, that's true, but I didn't mean that. Anyway, both of them are about 'expression.' For example, if someone has a profound dream beyond their personal abilities, is that dream vague? Is it a poor work of art like Picasso's?\" [4116] \"It seems like we're arguing about the meaning of the word 'art'...\"\n[4117] \"For me, 'dream' and 'beauty' are synonyms.\" [4118] \"Speaking of beauty...\"\n[4119] When Wakatsuki said that the culprit put the note in Sasaki's pocket, Kishida tried to say, \"Maybe not. Maybe Sasaki had the note with him all the time, didn't he? Even though the message was written, he might have met the person he wanted to talk to by chance and spoken to them, and the note was put in his pocket and carried with him...\"\n[4120] That's it! After Wakatsuki said that Sasaki, who had a sore right shoulder, wouldn't put the note in his right hip pocket, I focused my thoughts on this, but I wanted to refute Kishida's argument. Here's the counter-argument I didn't have a chance to say: \"Kishida-san, please wait! You said...that Sasaki met the person he wanted to talk to unexpectedly, when and where was that? The message was written after I doodled, around 4 PM, so you mean that after that, Sasaki met someone somewhere, right?\" [4121] That's what I wanted to ask him. Hikari stayed in his hotel room from 4 pm until we went out together at 6 pm. He didn't go out to find anyone, and no one visited him. So, what I wanted to say was: \"Hikari couldn't have met the person he was talking to unexpectedly!\" [4122] What a waste of my trying to remember! I sighed, feeling something stuck in my chest. [4123] \"Wait...,\" I murmured softly so no one could hear. [4124] We all inadvertently overlooked an obvious thing. How did Hikari, who wrote the message to X, get it to X's hands? After writing the message, Hikari didn't meet anyone outside the hotel, did he? [4125] I couldn't hear any sound from anyone or anything. Because I finally felt that the truth was gradually approaching. [4126] 3\n[4127] How did Hikari manage to deliver the note to someone? I started with this question, asking everyone to think together. \"It's indeed strange. Why didn't we notice it before?\" Nishijima took up the question first. [4128] Indeed, why didn't we notice it before? [4129] \"Hikari wrote the message at four o'clock, and we left the hotel at six o'clock. During that time, Hikari didn't have any contact with anyone outside the hotel. What did he intend to do with the written note?\" [4130] In the end, it was Fujimori who gave the answer, \"Well, this... The message that says to meet at seven o'clock or nine o'clock would not have been written at four o'clock and still be in his hand by six o'clock. He could have just been daydreaming and the time for the appointment would have come.\" [4131]\"Yes, yes...!\" Right! [4132]\"This means that the secret meeting is not urgent. The meeting time is 7:00, and it may refer to the next day's 7:00!\" [4133]What? [4134]\"So, if the note is not given to the other person after 7:00 on the day it was written, the other person will misunderstand the date. That's why the note written at 4:00 is still in his hand by 6:00.\" [4135]No! Takano has completely lost track and didn't even notice the reasoning laid out earlier. I don't want the conversation to go this way. [4136] \"Aya-senpai, I think that's not right.\" [4137] \"Why?\" [4138] Aya seems surprised. He must be very satisfied with the new discovery that the meeting time on the note is actually the next day! [4139] \"That's strange! Because the point that 'the right-shouldered Suguro will put the note paper in his buttock pocket' is wrong! Didn't you say that? 'The killer put the note paper in his pocket' was the conclusion we reached just now, right? So, the note paper has already been handed over to the killer!\" [4140] \"...\"\n[4141] \"Moreover, if the appointment is scheduled for the next day, I think Sugiyama would clearly write the date, and there's no need to force himself to write when he's in pain.\" [4142] \"\u2026\"\n[4143] \"What do you think? \" [4144] \"I know. You're right.\" The senior brother blushed and laughed, feeling that his glorious era had completely passed. [4145] \"So, Hizaki, what do you think?\" Asaeda senior brother rubbed his shoulder while listening to our conversation. [4146] I hadn't quite gathered my thoughts, but I could only speak as I thought, \"Actually, the note has already been delivered to the killer, but the problem is, how did Shihori send the note? Because until the time of the appointment, he didn't leave the hotel. If he could still deliver the note, he must have asked someone else to do it.\" [4147] At this point, Hirata seemed to suddenly think of something and said, \"Ask someone else to deliver... Could it be that he asked the innkeeper's help? The innkeeper was indeed asked to help deliver the letter. That was after 4 pm, wasn't it?\" [4148] \"Yes, that's right...\"\n[4149] \"But, we don't know the content of the letter that the innkeeper delivered, do we?\" Asaeda again poured cold water on the conversation, \"It was addressed to a Tokyo publishing house...\"\n[4150] \"Mr. Yamamoto, the editor-in-chief?\" [4151] \"Yes. Because of the landslide, the letter didn't get sent out, so it was recovered by the police and not delivered to anyone, right? The only letter the innkeeper was keeping at that time was that one. If Sagara still asked the innkeeper to deliver another letter and asked her to give it to a certain gentleman in the village, the innkeeper should have informed the police officers.\" [4152] \"Hmm, so that doesn't hold up. If there was another letter, the innkeeper would have testified, and it's generally not a practice to entrust secretive letters to someone else.\" [4153] \"You just said 'he must have asked someone to deliver it', right?\" [4154] \"Asking someone to deliver it must have a way. Sagara probably didn't commission someone in an obvious way because he and the murderer both wanted to establish contact cautiously. When the phone call came, you couldn't even tell the gender, right?\" [4155] \"Ah... you mean the frog-like voice?\"\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask."